+<exercises xml:id="exercises-welcome-to-matlab">
+	<title>💻 Coding Exercises</title>
+
+	<exercise>
+		<statement>
+			<p>
+				Which of the following are valid and useable variable names? Explain your answers.
+				<ol>
+					<li>
+						Homework 1
+					</li>
+					<li>
+						1Homework
+					</li>
+					<li>
+						Homework\#1
+					</li>
+					<li>
+						Homework\_1
+					</li>
+					<li>
+						HoMeWoRkNuMbEr1
+					</li>
+				</ol>
+			</p>
+		</statement>
+	</exercise>
+
+	<exercise>
+		<statement>
+			<p>
+				Compute the following using the correct order of operations
+				<ol>
+					<li>
+						<m>2 - 4*(5^{3-2} + 2*(5+6))</m>
+					</li>
+					<li>
+						<m>2 - \displaystyle \frac{4*5^{3-2}}{2*(5+6)}</m>
+					</li>
+					<li>
+						<m>2 - \displaystyle \frac{4*5^{3-2}}{\frac{2}{5}+6}</m>
+					</li>
+				</ol>
+			</p>
+		</statement>
+	</exercise>
+
+	<exercise>
+		<statement>
+			<p>
+				The volume of a truncated pyramid with a square base is given by
+				<me>
+					V = \frac{h}{3}\left(a^2 + b^2 + ab\right)
+				</me>
+				where <m>h</m> is the height, <m>a</m> is the length of one of the sides of the base and <m>b</m> is the length of the sides of one of the top (also a square).  Find the volume if <m>a=5, b=3, h=10</m> by first defining <m>a, b</m> and <m>h</m> as separate variables and then defining <m>V</m> in terms of them.
+			</p>
+		</statement>
+	</exercise>
+
+	<exercise>
+		<statement>
+			<p>
+				The escape velocity from a planet is given by <m>v~=~\sqrt{\displaystyle\frac{2GM}{R}}</m> where <m>G</m> is the gravitational constant, <m>M</m> is the mass and <m>R</m> is the radius of the planet.  Compute the escape velocity of both earth and the moon using an online source to find the constants.  In each case, first define <m>G,M</m> and <m>R</m> and then define <m>v</m> in terms of them.  Make sure to give the citation of your source.
+			</p>
+		</statement>
+	</exercise>
+
+	<exercise>
+		<statement>
+			<p>
+				Stirling's formula for computing the factorial is <m>n! \approx \sqrt{2\pi n} \left(\displaystyle \frac{n}{e} \right)^n</m>.  For each of <m>n=1,2,3,\cdots, 20</m>, compute both sides of this approximation.  Hint: MATLAB provides the built-in function <c>factorial(n)</c> which computes <m>n!</m>.
+				Note that this problem will be much easier to do with a loop which we will do later.
+			</p>
+		</statement>
+	</exercise>
+
+	<exercise>
+		<statement>
+			<p>
+				In considering Stirling's formula, since the numbers grow so quickly in using the factorial, it can be advantageous to use the natural logarithm function.  A related approximation is given by <m>\ln (n!) \approx n \ln n - n</m>.  Compute the difference <m>\ln (n!) -( n \ln n - n)</m> for <m>n = 10, 20, 30, 40, 50</m>.  What is the largest value of <m>n</m> that MATLAB will allow in this difference calculation?
+				Note that this problem will be much easier to do with a loop which we will do later.
+			</p>
+		</statement>
+	</exercise>
+
+	<exercise xml:id="coding-matlab-desktop">
+		<title>MATLAB Desktop</title>
+
+		<statement>
+			<p>
+				Match each MATLAB Desktop panel with its purpose.
+			</p>
+			<p>
+				<ol>
+					<li>Command Window</li>
+					<li>Workspace</li>
+					<li>Files</li>
+					<li>Editor</li>
+				</ol>
+			</p>
+			<p>
+				<ol marker="A">
+					<li>Shows the current working directory and the files saved there.</li>
+					<li>Displays variables currently stored in memory, including their names and values.</li>
+					<li>Used to write, edit, and debug MATLAB programs and scripts.</li>
+					<li>Where you type commands at the <c>&gt;&gt;</c> prompt and see results.</li>
+				</ol>
+			</p>
+		</statement>
+	</exercise>
+
+	<exercise xml:id="coding-command-window">
+		<title>Command Window</title>
+
+		<statement>
+			<p>
+				Enter each of the following expressions in the Command Window, one at a time. After each one, write down the value of <c>ans</c>.
+			</p>
+			<p>
+				<ol>
+					<li><c>3 + 4</c></li>
+					<li><c>ans * 2</c></li>
+					<li><c>10 - ans</c></li>
+					<li><c>ans^2</c></li>
+				</ol>
+			</p>
+			<p>
+				Explain why <c>ans</c> changes after each command.
+			</p>
+		</statement>
+	</exercise>
+
+	<exercise xml:id="coding-command-variables">
+		<title>Variables</title>
+
+		<statement>
+			<p>
+				Predict the values of <c>a</c>, <c>b</c>, and <c>c</c> after the following sequence of commands is executed. Then verify your predictions by running the commands in MATLAB.
+			</p>
+			<program language="matlab">
+				<code>
+					a = 5;
+					b = a + 3;
+					a = 10;
+					c = a + b;
+				</code>
+			</program>
+			<p>
+				Does changing <c>a</c> to <m>10</m> on the third line affect the value of <c>b</c>? Why or why not?
+			</p>
+		</statement>
+	</exercise>
+
 </exercises>
\ No newline at end of file
